#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "2acc65a4-62df-5e61-a3c9-cb8a08e1ff8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9831" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W$, and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 18 \\\\ 18 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwe pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(WA)^{T} \\cdot WAx &= (WA)^{T} \\cdot Wb \\\\\nA^{T}(W^{T}W)Ax &= A^{T}(W^{T}W)b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}29 \\\\ 54\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+54 = 104\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "22ff2997-c3c3-560e-afdc-52da342dcc10" :question/math-academy-id "e-9832" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W$, and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwe pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(WA)^{T} \\cdot WAx &= (WA)^{T} \\cdot Wb \\\\\nA^{T}(W^{T}W)Ax &= A^{T}(W^{T}W)b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 \\\\ -21 & 21\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-39 \\\\ 39\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ -21 & 21 & 39\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{13}{7}+x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{13}{7} + x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}]}
